/**
 * Moodle variables
 *
 * Variables written for Moodle specific components
 *
 * Please do not override any Bootstrap variables here, custom Bootstrap variable should go in
 * preset files instead.
 */
/**
 * Colour mode helpers.
 *
 * Imported before the component partials so that they can each carry their own dark mode overrides next to the rules
 * those overrides correct. The dark palette itself lives in dark.scss, which is imported last.
 */
/**
 * Emit the content as a dark colour mode override of the rule it is written inside.
 *
 * Bootstrap's own color-mode() mixin puts the [data-bs-theme] selector at the point it is included, so nesting it
 * inside a rule produces a descendant selector and the attribute is on the html tag, an ancestor. This prefixes the
 * enclosing selector instead, which both matches and inherits that selector's specificity, so an override always
 * wins against the rule it belongs to without the ancestors having to be repeated by hand.
 *
 * selector-nest() rather than interpolating the parent directly: interpolation would paste a comma separated parent
 * in as one string and only the first of its selectors would get the attribute, letting the rest leak into light
 * mode.
 *
 * Use it beside the rule being corrected:
 *
 *     .my-panel {
 *         background-color: $gray-100;
 *
 *         @include dark-mode {
 *             background-color: var(--bs-gray-200);
 *         }
 *     }
 */
/**
 * Lighten a colour until it is readable on a dark surface.
 *
 * The theme colours are chosen for a white page and are mostly too dark to use as text once the page is dark. This
 * tints them in 5% steps and returns the first tint which reaches the required contrast, so that the dark palette
 * keeps meeting WCAG AA even when a site changes the brand colour.
 *
 * @param {Color} $color The colour to lighten.
 * @param {Color} $background The surface the colour is drawn on.
 * @param {Number} $min The contrast ratio to reach.
 * @return {Color}
 */
